WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.009
Whether it's introducing an iconic character,
creating a mind-blowing battle, or surprising

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:09.840
fans with the unexpected, first impressions
are key to a successful superhero cinematic

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:10.500
universe.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.240
With that in mind, let's take a look at some
of the genre's greatest opening scenes.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:15.760
Batman

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:21.260
These days, Batman is everywhere, but in 1989,
comic book fans were still waiting for the

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.420
Dark Knight take over the silver screen.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.440
They were also ready for something darker
than TV's Batman; something that felt more

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.220
like the comics written by Alan Moore and
Frank Miller.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.660
Director Tim Burton was able to impress even
the most diehard fans with his Gothic take

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.350
on Gotham City in this opening scene—a city
cloaked in darkness, with buildings that looked

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.900
like they'd been designed by a drunken Fritz
Lang.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.059
Enter, Batman.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.900
Two thugs, having recently mugged a family,
are busy counting their ill-gotten cash when

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.180
But bullets can't stop Batman.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.840
And then we get to hear the most iconic superhero
line of all time

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:08.960
"What are you?"

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.780
"I'm batman."

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.340
Just like that, a cinematic hero is born.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:15.900
Blade

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.880
Long before the MCU existed, there was 1998's
Blade: the first successful Marvel movie.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.840
Directed by Stephen Norrington, the film’s
opening fight scene was hailed by critics

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.740
as "the best set piece in comic book history."

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.440
A man is lured inside the world's nastiest
nightclub by a beautiful woman, and we witness

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.960
his revulsion as he realizes the ceiling is
rigged with blood-spurting sprinklers.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.380
Then they start baring fangs, and we realize
this poor sap is about to become dinner.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.259
But before the feast begins, in comes a sword-wielding
superhero: the half-human, half-vampire Blade,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.820
played by Wesley Snipes.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.800
He stakes, blasts and cuts with an impressive
array of weapons.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.420
This opening grabs your attention right off
the bat.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.220
Spider-Man 2

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.340
What makes Tobey Maguire's Spider-Man so relatable
is that underneath the costume, he’s just

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.320
an ordinary guy with ordinary problems.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.840
Directed by Sam Raimi, the film finds Peter
Parker on the verge of losing his job at a

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:11.760
pizza restaurant.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.340
With only seven and a half minutes to make
a career-saving delivery, Peter changes into

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.160
his Spidey suit.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.579
"He stole that guy's pizzas!"

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:26.129
Of course, Spider-Man can’t help but rescue
some kids from oncoming truck, but he loses

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:28.220
his job when he delivers the pizzas late.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.820
Being super isn't all it's cracked up to be,
especially when you're broke, lonely, and

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.820
tired of saving everyone but yourself.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:36.760
The Dark Knight

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.900
The opening to 2008's The Dark Knight is one
of the all-time greats.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:46.040
Filmed on 70mm IMAX, Christopher Nolan’s
masterpiece begins with a creeping score that

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:50.720
warns us something's up... just before a bunch
of thugs wearing clown masks kick off an elaborate

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.220
heist.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.340
They take over a bank, bantering about the
Joker, their enigmatic boss.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.820
As they work, they pick each other off, one
by one, until there's only one thug standing.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.140
Before he can get away, an injured mob enforcer
asks him:

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.440
"Honor… respect.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:07.280
Look at you?

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.900
What do you believe in, huh?"

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.900
That's when the clown pulls off his mask to
reveal the horrific face of the Joker.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.620
The Clown Prince of Crime has been there the
whole time!

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.980
As he prepares to make his getaway, he delivers
his chilling credo:

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:26.540
"I believe whatever doesn't kill you makes
you stranger."

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:28.680
Watchmen

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.300
Say what you want about the movie as a whole,
but there's no denying that the opening to

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.680
Zack Snyder’s Watchmen perfectly captures
the bloody beat-down of Alan Moore's graphic

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:38.180
novel.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.080
We open on the Comedian, a so-called superhero
who's done more dirty deeds than derring-do.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:48.100
The grizzled nihilist is alone, contemplating
life's many sick jokes, when a mysterious

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.660
opponent storms into the room.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.140
"Just a matter of time I suppose."

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.220
As the shadowy assassin beats the Comedian
into oblivion, Snyder employs his trademark

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:57.720
slow-mo.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:02.220
The entire fight is underscored by Nat King
Cole's "Unforgettable," and as the Comedian

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.320
is tossed out the window, we're left with
an opening no superhero fan will ever forget.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:07.840
Deadpool

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.160
We all know that Deadpool doesn't play by
the rules, and neither does the opening of

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.420
his R-rated blockbuster.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.439
Who would've thought that a movie about a
deformed mutant assassin would open with Juice

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.000
Newton's "Angel of the Morning"?

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.160
The song plays as the camera winds through
a suspended tableau of destruction, while

00:04:23.169 --> 00:04:27.960
the hilarious credits tell us to watch out
for "God's Perfect Idiot," a "Hot Chick,"

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.200
and a "British Villain."

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.280
Of course, the awesomeness doesn't stop there.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.440
Immediately after the credits, we see the
Merc with a Mouth perched on an overpass,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.260
jamming to "Shoop".

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.159
Deadpool breaks the fourth wall to say hello
to the audience

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.820
"Oh Oh Hello."

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.180
before jumping off the overpass and landing
in an SUV full of villains.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.580
We get to watch the slow-mo credits play out
in real time, gory and hysterically over-the-top.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.060
It sets the perfect tone for the madness to
follow.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.120
"Did i leave the stove on?"

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.020
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.660
When your 2017 movie starts with a 1980s Kurt
Russell, you know you're doing something right.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:09.520
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2 opens with
Ego the Living Planet and Meredith Quill cruising

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.770
down back roads of Missouri.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.840
We’re caught up in the romance of it all—not
to mention the amazing digital work done on

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:15.600
Russell's face.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.200
The lazy afternoon doesn't last long, though.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.180
"showtime a-holes"

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.700
34 years into the future, everyone’s favorite
a-holes are preparing for battle and, as you

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.280
can expect, bickering.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.160
"My nipples hurt oh goodness me"

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.100
When an oversized space squid comes tearing
out of the sky, the ensuing fight is accompanied

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.820
by Electric Light Orchestra blasting on the
stereo.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.240
Baby Groot dancing to ELO is possibly the
most adorable scene in the entire Marvel Cinematic

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.580
Universe—one that took two whole years of
planning.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.680
Really, the only thing better than watching
Baby Groot boogie is watching director James

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.520
Gunn choreograph all the dance moves.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:54.940
Thanks for watching!

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.280
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.750
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

